Keep in mind that the game doesn't have to make sense. In fact it
intentionally does not make sense in several areas. The example I like
to use the most is that elves can only be chaotic. "Because the game
draws on more than one source". Which of course would seem to indicate
that they could be more than just chaotic - the argument being used
actually contrary to the fact that they have clearly only drawn on one
source and therefore elves can only be chaotic. Various other
ridiculous arguments are used to support this "position", one being a
challenge of the "tolkein" mythology (which is ridiculous in and of
itself considering the dozens of direct references to tolkein, both
with items like sting and orcrist, hobbits, and of course the "more
info" entry for elves itself).. Not to mention of course that even if
you bar tolkein mythology there is always mythology from other worlds
like Dragonlance, which is arguably as popular (or more) than tolkein
mythology, and that's not saying tolkein is not the standard or
anything, which like it or not, it is. Same with the ranger class.

There are many possible reasons why the devteam might want to do
things this way, although I can't think of any myself.

